query:
Show that there exist scalar functions $p(s),q(s)$ such that
\begin{align}
e^{sU}=p(s)\,I+q(s)\,U,
\label{eq:exp-reduce}
\end{align}
and derive the coupled linear ODE system
\begin{align}
p'(s)=...,\qquad q'(s)=...,
\qquad
p(0)=1,\ q(0)=0.
\label{eq:pq-ode}
\end{align}

Using \eqref{eq:frechet-exp} and the reduction \eqref{eq:exp-reduce},
show that the Fr\'echet derivative can be written in the \emph{four-term noncommutative basis}
\begin{align}
D(e^U)[E] \;=\; A\,E \;+\; B\,E\,U \;+\; C\,U\,E \;+\; D\,U\,E\,U,
\label{eq:frechet-basis}
\end{align}
for scalar coefficients $A,B,C,D$ depending only on $(t,d)$.
Then compute $A,B,C,D$ explicitly in terms of $\lambda_\pm$.

ground_truth:
\begin{align}
B&=C
=\frac{1}{(\Delta\lambda)^2}\left[
\frac{(\lambda_++\lambda_-)\,e^{\lambda_-}\big(e^{\Delta\lambda}-1\big)}{\Delta\lambda}
-\lambda_+e^{\lambda_-}-\lambda_-e^{\lambda_+}
\right],
\\[6pt]
D&=\frac{1}{(\Delta\lambda)^2}\left[
e^{\lambda_+}+e^{\lambda_-}
-\frac{2e^{\lambda_-}\big(e^{\Delta\lambda}-1\big)}{\Delta\lambda}
\right],
\\[6pt]
A&=\frac{1}{(\Delta\lambda)^2}\left[
\lambda_+^2 e^{\lambda_-}+\lambda_-^2 e^{\lambda_+}
-\frac{2\lambda_+\lambda_-\,e^{\lambda_-}\big(e^{\Delta\lambda}-1\big)}{\Delta\lambda}
\right],
\end{align}
where $\Delta\lambda\equiv\lambda_+-\lambda_-$.
